# November 28, 2026: Carnegie libraries of California, part 1: the towns A to G on Wikipedia's list (part 2 takes H to O
# and part 3 P to Z). A batch of the pass the owner asked for to put the buildings Andrew Carnegie paid for on the map. No
# Carnegie library in these towns was on the Atlas before. Pomona College's library, one of the state's two college
# Carnegie libraries, is here under Claremont; the other, at Mills College in Oakland, is left to part 2.
# Left out for want of a site: Chula Vista, Concord, Covina, Dinuba and East San Diego, all pulled down (see the note).
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia's list of Carnegie libraries in California and its articles; the National
# Register cover document California Carnegie Libraries (1990, the Park Service's copy) and the Register form for Dixon;
# the Pacific Coast Architecture Database (University of Washington) for Azusa, Coalinga and Glendale; the Fresno County
# Public Library's history; Fullerton History; KVPR's piece on the Carnegie libraries of the Central Valley; the Tulare
# County Library's Dinuba page; and the own pages of the museums, libraries and arts centre that use the buildings.
# Pins: ArcGIS geocoder points for street addresses and street corners, checked against Wikipedia's points where it gives
# them, and OpenStreetMap points for three buildings; every pin looked at on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'carnegie_caa_hwrt','label':'Carnegie libraries, California (A to G)','added':'2026-11-28 05:40'}
LK=W('List of Carnegie libraries in California')
MP=['National Register cover document: California Carnegie Libraries (1990)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/a0edf010-9916-4cb9-a4ff-59d0c7ab4fc1']
PCAD='Pacific Coast Architecture Database'
PLACES=[
N('alameda-carnegie-library-ca','Alameda Carnegie Library (old Alameda Free Library)','Alameda','ca','civic','standing',37.76595,-122.24361,'exact','view','walk','','1902–1998',
  "Carnegie gave Alameda $35,000 on October 3, 1899, and the library was built in 1902 and 1903, the first building of the city's own library, which had moved from place to place since 1877. Wikipedia's article names the architects as C. H. Foster and his son G. J. Foster; the list names Wilcox and Curtis. It mixes Neoclassical, Renaissance and Baroque Revival, and the 1990 state survey thought it probably the largest of California's temple-fronted Carnegie libraries. It was the public library until 1998, and the list says it has stood empty since; a new main library opened on Oak Street in 2006. On the National Register since 1982.",
  'Two storeys of pressed and moulded buff brick over a grey sandstone basement, a porch with two brick columns (said to be the first of their kind in California), and an open book carved in the pediment. Inside, a marble stair leads up to the main room, which has five stained glass windows on its south wall and a raised glass skylight in the metal roof; the inside was not checked as it is now.',
  '','2264 Santa Clara Avenue, across the street from the old Alameda City Hall. Empty when last reported: look from the street.',
  [W('Alameda Free Library'),LK,MP],brick='no',bcol='buff',conf='mixed'),
N('alturas-carnegie-library-ca','Alturas Carnegie Library','Alturas','ca','civic','standing',41.48325,-120.54173,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1947',
  "Carnegie granted Alturas $10,000 on November 20, 1908. F. J. DeLongchamps drew the library, which opened in 1918 and was the library until 1947. A private owner later remade it as an office building, inside and out, so that the 1990 state survey found it looking more like the Moderne style than the classical building it was; the county then leased it back for offices. Wikipedia's list says it now holds law offices.",
  '',
  '','201 South Court Street. Privately owned offices: look from the street.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('anaheim-carnegie-library-ca','Anaheim Carnegie Library (Muzeo)','Anaheim','ca','civic','standing',33.83361,-117.91361,'exact','open','walk','','1907–1963',
  "Carnegie granted Anaheim $10,000 on February 6, 1907. John C. Austin drew the Classical Revival library, built in 1908 and opened in 1909. It was the public library until 1963 and the city's personnel office in the 1970s, and since 1987 it has been part of a museum, now Muzeo, a museum and cultural centre built around it. Wikipedia calls it the only Carnegie library left in Orange County; those in Fullerton, Orange, Santa Ana and Huntington Beach are gone. On the National Register since 1979.",
  '',
  '','241 South Anaheim Boulevard, downtown. Muzeo, run as a museum and cultural centre; tickets are sold through its own site. It was closed for a new installation in late November 2026. Hours from its own page.',
  [W('Carnegie Library (Anaheim, California)'),['Muzeo','https://muzeo.org/'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Wed–Sun 11–5'),
N('antioch-carnegie-library-ca','Antioch Carnegie Library','Antioch','ca','civic','standing',38.01423,-121.81236,'exact','view','walk','','1915–1967',
  "One of three small Carnegie libraries in Contra Costa County, with Concord and Walnut Creek, each paid for by a $2,500 grant (Antioch's on June 1, 1915) and built with the county library, the town and townspeople together. Francis Reid drew it in a Neoclassical style, and it was the library from 1916 to 1967. The 1990 state survey found it a museum and the home of the historical society; Wikipedia's list says it is now a church.",
  '',
  '','519 F Street. Now a church, says Wikipedia\'s list; when it opens was not checked. Look from the street.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('auburn-carnegie-library-ca','Auburn Carnegie Library (Old Auburn Library)','Auburn','ca','civic','standing',38.89975,-121.07200,'exact','view','walk','','1907–1968',
  "Carnegie granted Auburn $10,000 on March 9, 1907. Allen D. Fellows drew the Classical Revival library, which was finished in 1909 and was the library from May 26, 1909, to 1968; the county library is now on Nevada Street. The 1990 state survey counted it among the essentially unaltered temple-fronted Carnegie libraries and found it an art and senior centre; Wikipedia's list says it is an arts centre. On the National Register since 2011.",
  '',
  '','175 Almond Street. An arts centre; when it opens was not checked. Look from the street.',
  [W('Auburn Public Library (California)'),LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('bayliss-carnegie-library-ca','Bayliss Carnegie Library (Bayliss District Library)','Bayliss','ca','civic','standing',39.58279,-122.04673,'exact','open','drive','','1916–',
  "A travelling library of the Glenn County Library first served the farms of the Bayliss Tract, and the people there wanted a building of their own. Carnegie granted $4,000 on January 5, 1916, the county promised the tax money he asked for, an irrigation company gave the land, and University of California classes planned the planting. W. H. Weeks drew it, and it opened in 1917. The 1990 state survey called it perhaps the only Carnegie library in the country built at a country crossroads, in a place that was neither a town nor a district; volunteers staffed it then, and it is still a library.",
  'A one-storey building with no basement, its rooms divided by wide arches.',
  '','7830 County Road 39, in open farm country near Glenn. A public library; its hours were not found on a page of whoever runs it.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('beaumont-carnegie-library-ca','Beaumont Carnegie Library (Beaumont Library)','Beaumont','ca','civic','standing',33.93211,-116.98098,'exact','open','walk','','1913–',
  "Beaumont voted itself a library district on August 12, 1911, a year before it became a city, and the library first had two rooms in a bank. Carnegie granted $10,000 in 1913 (April 28, says Wikipedia's list); E. L. Hopkins drew the Neoclassical building, begun in January 1914 and opened in June. Artists paid by the Works Progress Administration painted murals inside in the 1930s, among them Henri De Kruif's mural of early Beaumont of 1936. A wing that more than doubled it was added in 1965 (1966, says the list) and a community room in 1981, and a mansard roof was put over old and new parts alike, so the 1990 state survey found it no longer looked like a temple-fronted Carnegie library. Wikipedia calls it the last Carnegie library left in Riverside County. It is still the library.",
  'The 1930s murals, which Wikipedia says are still in excellent condition.',
  '','125 East 8th Street. A public library, run by its own library district; its page tells of a building project to enlarge and renovate it. Hours from the library\'s page.',
  [W('Beaumont Library District'),['Beaumont Library District','https://mybld.org/'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Mon–Sat 9–6; Sun 1–5',conf='mixed'),
N('biggs-carnegie-library-ca','Biggs Carnegie Library','Biggs','ca','civic','standing',39.41353,-121.71037,'exact','open','walk','','1906–',
  "Carnegie granted Biggs $5,000 on April 23, 1906, the smallest grant for a town library in California and a large one for a place of fewer than 500 people. The library was built in 1908. The 1990 state survey found it still the public library, with the city's offices in the basement, and Wikipedia's list still counts it a library.",
  'A small brick building, one storey over a raised basement, nearly a cube under a low hip roof, noted for its brickwork. Two thirds of the porch across the front has been glassed in.',
  '','464 B Street. A public library; its hours were not found on a page of whoever runs it.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('calexico-carnegie-library-ca','Calexico Carnegie Library','Calexico','ca','civic','standing',32.66955,-115.49321,'exact','open','walk','','1915–',
  "Calexico asked for $25,000; Carnegie gave $10,000 (March 16, 1915, says Wikipedia's list), judging the town too small for more, so Allison and Allison of Los Angeles simplified their Spanish Colonial Revival design. It was built in 1918 and was the library until a new one opened in 1986. The city then used it for storage until it was restored in 2007, and it is now the Carnegie Technology Center, a branch of the city library with classes in English, citizenship, computers and art. On the National Register since 2005.",
  'A tile hip roof, and a front door between pilasters under a carved scroll.',
  '','420 Heber Avenue. A city library branch. The library\'s page said both would be closed the week of November 23, 2026. Hours from the library\'s page.',
  [W('Calexico Carnegie Library'),['Calexico Public Library: Carnegie','https://www.calexicolibrary.org/carnegie'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Mon–Thu 3–7 p.m.'),
N('chico-carnegie-library-ca','Chico Carnegie Library (Chico History Museum)','Chico','ca','civic','standing',39.72946,-121.84227,'exact','open','walk','','1904–',
  "Carnegie granted Chico $10,000 on January 7, 1904. Stone and Smith of San Francisco drew a Richardsonian Romanesque library, built in 1905. In 1939 Louis Brouchoud of Chico remade it: he turned it to face another way, took off its tower and porch, and gave it a tile roof and decorative tile, so that it now reads as Mediterranean Revival. It has been a museum since 1986, now the Chico History Museum.",
  '',
  '','141 Salem Street, at 2nd Street, downtown, a few blocks from the university. A museum; it asks $5 an adult. Hours from its own page.',
  [['Chico History Museum: About','https://chicohistorymuseum.org/page-18241'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Thu–Sun 11–4'),
N('clovis-carnegie-library-ca','Clovis Carnegie Library','Clovis','ca','civic','standing',36.82611,-119.70177,'exact','view','walk','','1914–',
  "Carnegie granted Clovis $7,000 on March 11, 1914, one of the grants the Fresno County library arranged for its branches. The 1990 state survey says it was built in 1915; KVPR says 1914, and that it was the library until the 1970s. The survey says it stood empty a long while until townspeople restored it in 1985 as a hall upstairs and the Chamber of Commerce's offices below; KVPR says the chamber has been there since 1979. It is a Fresno County historic landmark. KVPR reported that it was threatened with demolition as recently as 2022; Wikipedia's list says the chamber is still there.",
  'A stucco building under a low hip roof, with a projecting entrance under its own hip roof, altered somewhat in 1985; KVPR calls it Prairie style.',
  '','325 Pollasky Avenue, in Old Town. The Chamber of Commerce, with a hall upstairs: look from the street.',
  [['KVPR: The Carnegie libraries of the Central Valley (2026)','https://www.kvpr.org/podcast/central-valley-roots/2026-01-28/the-carnegie-libraries-of-the-central-valley'],LK,MP],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('colton-carnegie-library-ca','Colton Carnegie Library (Colton Area Museum)','Colton','ca','civic','standing',34.06871,-117.32361,'exact','open','walk','','1906–',
  "Carnegie granted Colton $10,000 (April 10, 1906, says Wikipedia's list), and the town gave the site. Franklin P. Burnham of Los Angeles drew it and Kaiser and Loomis of Colton built it, and it opened on November 11, 1908, the only Neoclassical building in town. Besides the books it held meetings and clubs and even served as a church. The library moved out in 1982, the building was restored, and the Colton Area Museum opened in it in December 1991. On the National Register since 1988.",
  'A porch of Ionic columns under an ornamented pediment, pilasters at the corners, two fireplaces, and a skylight over a central rotunda.',
  '','380 North La Cadena Drive. A museum; special tours by appointment. Hours from its own page.',
  [W('Colton Carnegie Library'),['Colton Area Museum','http://coltonareamuseum.com/'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Wed and Fri 1–4; Sat 11–2'),
N('colusa-carnegie-library-ca','Colusa Carnegie Library','Colusa','ca','civic','standing',39.21365,-122.00991,'exact','view','walk','','1905–1964',
  "Carnegie granted Colusa $10,000 (January 19, 1905, says Wikipedia's list), Stone and Smith of San Francisco (Louis S. Stone and Henry C. Smith) drew it, the firm that also drew Chico's, and the library, built in 1906, cost $13,158 in all. It was one of 13 Carnegie libraries built in the Sacramento Valley between 1903 and 1917, and Wikipedia calls it the oldest of them that still looks as it did. It was the library until 1964 and has been the police station since 1977. A Colusa landmark since 1981 and on the National Register since 1990.",
  'A veneer of sandstone from Sites, a parapet, a metal pediment and entablature on pilasters, arched basement windows, and stone lintels carved with the names of famous authors.',
  '','260 6th Street. The city police department: look from the street.',
  [W('Colusa Carnegie Library'),LK,MP],brick='no'),
N('corning-carnegie-library-ca','Corning Carnegie Library','Corning','ca','civic','standing',39.92912,-122.18117,'exact','view','walk','','1913–',
  "Carnegie granted Corning $10,000 on August 11, 1913. The 1990 state survey counted it among the Mission and Spanish Revival Carnegie libraries, and found it privately owned and restored for offices, with its character kept but too few tenants.",
  '',
  '','618 4th Street. Privately owned: look from the street.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('dixon-carnegie-library-ca','Dixon Carnegie Library (Dixon Library)','Dixon','ca','civic','standing',38.44661,-121.82207,'area','open','walk','','1911–',
  "The Women's Improvement Club started the library in 1911 and wrote to Carnegie's office for a building; he granted $10,000 on November 21, 1911, but the deed was signed by the Dixon Union High School Library District, which bought the site for $10, making Dixon one of three California towns whose Carnegie library came through a high school district. Parker and Kenyon of San Francisco drew it, Brady and Fischer of San Francisco built it, and the cornerstone gives 1912. Additions at the back came in 1957 or 1958, 1989 and 1990, when the entrance moved round to North First Street. It is still the library, part of the Solano County Library since January 2020. On the National Register.",
  'A tall single storey over a raised basement, brick two bricks thick under smooth plaster, in a Mission style with simple classical touches, beside a city park; the additions behind are faced in brick. Inside are tall built-in bookcases under deep crown moulding.',
  '','135 East B Street, between North First and North Second Streets; the library now gives its address as 230 North First Street. A public library; its page gave only holiday hours when read, in November 2026.',
  [['National Register nomination: Dixon Carnegie Library','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/69a8adf2-78b3-4d10-969d-05e7cc00d2c1'],['Solano County Library: Dixon Library','https://solanolibrary.gov/hours-and-locations/dixon-library/'],LK,MP],
  brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('eagle-rock-carnegie-library-ca','Eagle Rock Carnegie Library (Center for the Arts Eagle Rock)','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.13963,-118.21491,'exact','open','walk','','1914–1981',
  "Eagle Rock was its own city when Carnegie granted it $7,500 on March 11, 1914, and its library opened in 1915 to plans by W. E. Kleinpell. Los Angeles took Eagle Rock in 1923, and in 1927 the library was rebuilt on its old foundations as a branch of the Los Angeles Public Library, to plans the Register gives to Henry C. Newton and Robert D. Murray. It closed in 1981 when a larger branch opened, and since 1997 it has been an arts centre, now the Center for the Arts Eagle Rock. A Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument since 1985 and on the National Register since 1987.",
  'A Mission and Spanish Colonial Revival building at the corner of Colorado Boulevard and Rockland.',
  '','2225 Colorado Boulevard, at Rockland, in Eagle Rock. An arts centre run by a community group. Hours from its own page.',
  [W('Center for the Arts Eagle Rock'),['Center for the Arts Eagle Rock','http://cfaer.org/'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Open for workshops and by appointment only',conf='mixed'),
N('eureka-carnegie-library-ca','Eureka Carnegie Library (Morris Graves Museum of Art)','Eureka','ca','civic','standing',40.80050,-124.16600,'exact','open','walk','','1901–',
  "Eureka was the first city to pay for a public library under California's library law of 1878. Carnegie granted it $20,000 on October 3, 1901, and after a competition Knowles Evans and B. C. Tarver of Eureka drew the Classical Revival library, built in 1902; the builder ran over budget, and Carnegie would not give the $10,000 more the trustees asked. The original dome came off. In 1996, with the building marked for demolition, the city and the Humboldt Arts Council saved it; it was restored from 1999 and opened on January 1, 2000, as the Morris Graves Museum of Art. On the National Register since 1986.",
  'Red brick and granite from the Mad River outside; inside, solid redwood columns two storeys tall ring a coloured tile mosaic floor in the rotunda, now lit by a skylight where the dome was.',
  '','636 F Street, in Old Town. An art museum of the Humboldt Arts Council: adults $5, children free, and free all day on the first Saturday of the month. Accessible parking in front on F Street; a lift reaches every floor. Hours from its own page.',
  [W('Carnegie Free Library (Eureka, California)'),['Morris Graves Museum of Art: Visit','https://humboldtarts.org/visit'],LK,MP],
  brick='some',bcol='red',hours='Wed–Sun 12–5; closed major holidays'),
N('exeter-carnegie-library-ca','Exeter Carnegie Library','Exeter','ca','civic','standing',36.29380,-119.13947,'area','view','walk','','1914–1976',
  "The town's Women's Club started a library in 1910. Carnegie granted $5,000 on May 8, 1914, one of six Carnegie libraries in Tulare County, and the library was built in 1916 to a Mission Revival design by A. Merrill Bowser, one of three sent to Carnegie's secretary. It was the library until 1976 and has been a community centre since. It is one of two Carnegie libraries left in Tulare County, with Orosi. On the National Register since 1990.",
  'A low hip roof, a gabled parapet, a wide entrance with glass panels beside the door, and a pattern like the Union Jack in the windows and the transom over the door.',
  '','309 South E Street, in a residential part of town. A community centre owned by the city: look from the street.',
  [W('Exeter Public Library'),LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('ferndale-carnegie-library-ca','Ferndale Carnegie Library (Ferndale Library)','Ferndale','ca','civic','standing',40.57990,-124.26120,'site','open','walk','','1908–',
  "Ferndale's reading rooms came and went from 1896, and the 1906 earthquake wrecked one of their buildings. In 1908 the city won an $8,000 Carnegie grant (March 21, says Wikipedia's list), and Warren Skellings of Eureka drew a small Classical Revival library of reinforced concrete on a 40-foot lot given on condition it be used for a library; Ackerman and Ackerman of Eureka built it for $7,775.40. It opened on April 2, 1910, with \"Ferndale Library\" over the door, Carnegie having asked that his name be left off. In 1912 concrete walls were built to keep a neighbour's cattle off the plantings. It became a branch of the county library in 1915, and a rear addition of 1977 kept its style. It is still the library, the only Carnegie library in north-western California still used as one. On the National Register since 1990.",
  'The original bookcases and furniture are still in use, Wikipedia says.',
  '','807 Main Street. A branch of the Humboldt County Library. Hours from the library\'s page.',
  [W('Ferndale Public Library'),['Humboldt County Library: locations and hours','https://flyacv.com/1348'],LK,MP],brick='no',hours='Tue and Thu 12–9; Wed 12–5; Fri–Sat 12–5'),
N('gilroy-carnegie-library-ca','Gilroy Carnegie Library (Gilroy Museum)','Gilroy','ca','civic','standing',37.00793,-121.57165,'exact','open','walk','','1906–',
  "Carnegie granted Gilroy $10,000 (March 12, 1906, says Wikipedia's list). William H. Weeks drew the Classical Revival library, one of his Greek-temple Carnegie libraries, on donated land, and it opened on July 27, 1910. Historical things were shown in the basement from 1958, and the Gilroy Museum was formally set up there in 1963. The library moved out to Sixth and Rosanna Streets in 1975, the city says (1976, says Wikipedia), and the museum has used the whole building since. Wikipedia's list calls it virtually unchanged. On the National Register since 1988.",
  'A temple front with pediment and columns.',
  '','195 5th Street. The city\'s museum; the city gives no admission fee and asks callers to telephone. Hours from the city\'s page.',
  [W('Gilroy Free Library'),['City of Gilroy: Gilroy Museum','https://www.cityofgilroy.org/386'],LK,MP],brick='?',hours='Tue and Thu 10–4; 1st and 2nd Sat 10–2',conf='mixed'),
N('grass-valley-carnegie-library-ca','Grass Valley Carnegie Library (Royce Branch)','Grass Valley','ca','civic','standing',39.21682,-121.06330,'exact','open','walk','','1915–',
  "Grass Valley's earlier libraries of 1860 and 1869 did not last; a church library opened in 1901 to all comers, and the city took it over about a decade later. It asked Carnegie for money in 1914 and got $15,000 in 1915 (January 6, says Wikipedia's list). San Francisco architect William Mooser was chosen over William Weeks, Welch Brothers and Hannemann of Oakland built it, and it was finished in 1916 on the lot at 207 Mill Street where the philosopher Josiah Royce was born. The 1990 state survey found it essentially unaltered. It is still a library, a branch of the Nevada County Library, renamed for Royce in 2005. On the National Register since 1992.",
  'A Classical Revival front of red brick with white pilasters, and a plaque of 2005 at the entrance about Royce.',
  '','207 Mill Street, near Neal Street, downtown. A public library; closed on county holidays. Hours from the county\'s page.',
  [W('Grass Valley Public Library'),['Nevada County Library: locations and hours','https://www.nevadacountyca.gov/297/Locations-Hours'],LK,MP],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='Mon 10–6; Tue–Wed 10–7; Thu–Fri 10–6; Sat 9–4'),
N('gridley-carnegie-library-ca','Gridley Carnegie Library','Gridley','ca','civic','standing',39.36371,-121.69680,'exact','view','walk','','1913–',
  "Carnegie granted Gridley $7,000 on January 2, 1913. Chester Cole of Chico drew the library, and the firm that built Grass Valley's, Welch Brothers and Hannemann of Oakland, built it. Wikipedia's list calls it Classical Revival; the 1990 state survey put it with the classical Carnegie libraries that borrow from the Mission style, found it essentially unaltered, and said the county owned it and it stood empty. The list says it is now a private office.",
  '',
  '','519 Kentucky Street. Offices: look from the street.',
  [LK,MP,W('Grass Valley Public Library')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('pomona-college-carnegie-building-claremont','Carnegie Hall (old Carnegie library), Pomona College','Claremont','ca','inst','standing',34.09812,-117.71543,'site','view','walk','','1905–1953',
  "One of only two college libraries Carnegie paid for in California. Pomona College was offered $40,000 on March 15, 1905, on condition that it raise as much again as an endowment for upkeep. It stands on a public park the town gave the college on condition that the library be free to the town and nothing else be built there. Franklin P. Burnham drew it in reinforced concrete; after a concrete hotel collapsed in Long Beach the plans were stiffened at the last minute, and the college paid the extra. The cornerstone was laid in 1906 and it opened in 1908. It served students and townspeople alike until a county library branch opened in Claremont in 1914, and stayed the college library until 1953, when it was made over for teaching departments; it was remodelled again in 1968.",
  'A Classical Revival temple front, of reinforced concrete.',
  '','On College Avenue, on the Pomona College campus. A college building: look from the campus walks.',
  [LK,MP],brick='no'),
N('azusa-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Azusa Carnegie Library','Azusa','ca','civic','gone',34.13466,-117.90556,'site','view','walk','','1908–1959',
  "Carnegie granted Azusa $10,000 on December 24, 1908. Norman F. Marsh drew a small Classical Revival library, built from 1908 to 1910; a drawing of its front was shown in a Cooper-Hewitt Museum exhibition on Carnegie libraries in the 1980s. It served the town from 1910 until 1959, when it was pulled down and the city hall built on its lot.",
  'Nothing of the library stands; Azusa City Hall is on its ground.',
  '','213 East Foothill Boulevard, at the corner of Azusa Avenue: the city hall. Nothing of the library is left.',
  [[PCAD+': Carnegie Library, Azusa','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/3613'],LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('berkeley-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Berkeley Carnegie Library','Berkeley','ca','civic','gone',37.86849,-122.26795,'area','view','walk','','1903–1929',
  "Carnegie granted Berkeley $40,000 on February 12, 1903. John Galen Howard drew the library, which opened in 1905 at Shattuck Avenue and Kittredge Street. It served for only 24 years and was pulled down in 1929, the first Carnegie library lost in California.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','Shattuck Avenue at Kittredge Street, downtown. Which corner it stood on was not found.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('coalinga-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Coalinga Carnegie Library','Coalinga','ca','civic','gone',36.14147,-120.36090,'site','view','walk','','1912–1955',
  "One of three California Carnegie libraries that came through a high school district, with Dixon and Vacaville. Carnegie granted $20,000 (January 27, 1912, says Wikipedia's list), Swartz, Hotchkin and Swartz drew it in a Mediterranean Revival style, and it was built in 1913 and 1914 and finished in September 1914. It was pulled down in 1955, and the town's newer library is on its site.",
  'Nothing of the Carnegie building stands.',
  '','305 North 4th Street, the address the architecture database gives for the library. Nothing of the Carnegie building is left.',
  [[PCAD+': Coalinga Public Library','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/7587'],LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('corona-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Corona Carnegie Library','Corona','ca','civic','gone',33.87332,-117.56665,'area','view','walk','','1905–1978',
  "Corona's library began as a temperance union reading room in 1893 and moved from one rented room to another until Carnegie gave $10,000 in March 1905, raised to $11,500. Franklin P. Burnham drew the Classical Revival library, the only one of its style in town, S. L. Bloom built it, and it was dedicated on July 2, 1906. A new library opened at Sixth and Main in 1971, and the old one stood boarded up for seven years while the city argued over it. It was put on the National Register in 1977 and pulled down in 1978; the 1990 state survey said no California Carnegie library had been lost since. The lot was still empty in 2010.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','Eighth Street at Main Street. Which corner it stood on was not found.',
  [W('Andrew Carnegie Library (Corona, California)'),LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('el-centro-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the El Centro Carnegie Library','El Centro','ca','civic','gone',32.79150,-115.55584,'site','view','walk','','1909–2016',
  "Carnegie granted El Centro $10,000 on February 13, 1909, for a Classical Revival library. Earthquakes in the late 1920s damaged it badly; it was wrapped in steel bands, plastered over and stripped of its classical trim, and a new wing was added at an angle, so that only the large recessed window, once the entrance, still showed the old shape. It was the library until the earthquake of 2010 damaged it again, and it was pulled down in 2016.",
  'Nothing of the Carnegie building stands.',
  '','539 State Street. Nothing of the library is left.',
  [LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('escondido-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Escondido Carnegie Library','Escondido','ca','civic','gone',33.12055,-117.07882,'area','view','walk','','1908–1956',
  "Carnegie granted Escondido $7,500 on June 29, 1908. Van Trees and Millar of Los Angeles drew a Classical Revival library, one of the few with \"Carnegie Library\" cut over the door, and it was built in 1910 on given land at Kalmia Street and Third Avenue. It was pulled down in 1956, and the library built in its place that year is now the Mathes Center. The town's first library building, of 1894, was moved to Grape Day Park in 1971.",
  'Nothing of the Carnegie building stands.',
  '','Kalmia Street at Third Avenue, downtown. Which corner it stood on was not found.',
  [W('Escondido Public Library'),LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('fresno-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Fresno Carnegie Library','Fresno','ca','civic','gone',36.73679,-119.79501,'area','view','walk','','1901–1959',
  "Carnegie gave Fresno $30,000 in 1901 (February 14, says Wikipedia's list) on condition that the city tax itself $3,000 a year to keep the library. Copeland and Dole of New York won the city's design competition with a large Classical Revival library, one of the earliest and costliest Carnegie libraries in California. The cornerstone was laid on December 8, 1902, and it was finished on April 2, 1904, on land given at 1330 Broadway. A new central library opened at Mariposa and N Streets in 1959, and the Carnegie building was pulled down that year.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','1330 Broadway, at Merced Street, downtown. Which corner it stood on was not found.',
  [['Fresno County Public Library: history','https://www.fresnolibrary.org/about/history.html'],['KVPR: The Carnegie libraries of the Central Valley (2026)','https://www.kvpr.org/podcast/central-valley-roots/2026-01-28/the-carnegie-libraries-of-the-central-valley'],LK,MP],brick='?'),
N('fullerton-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Fullerton Carnegie Library','Fullerton','ca','civic','gone',33.87239,-117.92209,'area','view','walk','','1905–1942',
  "Carnegie granted Fullerton $10,000 on December 8, 1905. Hunt and Eager drew a library in the Mission and Spanish Colonial Revival styles, which opened in 1907 at the north-west corner of Wilshire and Pomona Avenues. It was pulled down in 1942 for a new library on the same corner, which now houses the Fullerton Museum Center.",
  'Nothing of the Carnegie building stands; the 1942 library that replaced it does.',
  '','The north-west corner of Wilshire and Pomona Avenues, downtown, where the Fullerton Museum Center stands. Nothing of the Carnegie building is left.',
  [['Fullerton History: then and now, Carnegie library and Fullerton Museum Center (2024)','https://fullertonhistory.com/2024/05/01/photographs-then-now-carnegie-library-fullerton-museum-center/'],LK],brick='?'),
N('glendale-carnegie-library-site-ca','Site of the Glendale Carnegie Library','Glendale','ca','civic','gone',34.14445,-118.25135,'area','view','walk','','1911–1977',
  "Glendale's first library was in a made-over pool hall in 1906. Carnegie offered $12,500 (August 11, 1911, says Wikipedia's list), Paul V. Tuttle of Los Angeles drew a one-storey Classical Revival library, and T. H. Addison, a local builder, put it up in 1913 and 1914 for $10,800. It was dedicated on November 13, 1914, at Kenwood Street and Fifth Street, later renamed Harvard Street. Lindley and Selkirk added to it in 1924, and two wings followed in 1940 to 1942, one a children's room. It was used until 1973 and pulled down in 1977, once the new library nearby was open.",
  'Nothing of the library stands.',
  '','Kenwood Street at Harvard Street, downtown. Which corner it stood on was not found.',
  [[PCAD+': Glendale Carnegie Library','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/5447'],LK,MP],brick='?'),
]
